The problem asks whether it is possible to travel from square 1 to square $N$ given a set of constraints.
We have $N$ squares labeled $1$ to $N$.
Some squares are "bad". A square $j$ is bad if it falls within any of the given intervals $[L_i, R_i]$.
We can move from square $x$ to $x+i$ if:
1. $A \leq i \leq B$
2. $x+i \leq N$
3. Square $x+i$ is not bad.

We need to determine if $N$ is reachable from 1.

Constraints:
$N$ can be up to $10^{12}$.
$M$ (number of bad intervals) is up to $2 \times 10^4$.
$A, B$ are small, $1 \leq A \leq B \leq 20$.
The intervals are disjoint and sorted: $R_i < L_{i+1}$.
Also $1 < L_i \leq R_i < N$. This means square 1 is never bad, and square $N$ is never bad.

Since $N$ is very large ($10^{12}$), we cannot simply run a BFS or DP on all squares from 1 to $N$. The state space is too big.
